Optional seed x86_64 Linux routes, v2.

This route creates its own fresh M and runs lex/read/names/check/lower before
x86 generation. It never invokes gn-run, ly-run, out-build or the project
linker on that M. These are phase-local reservations; the adopted manifest,
MEMORY-MAP and bootstrap closure are unchanged.

Absolute header cells, reserved from xg-build/xr-build through output:
192 = current FN index
193 = sole exported main FN index
194 = positive artifact fuel budget
195 = ELF RW segment file offset
196 = profile (0 standalone scalar ELF, 1 context-v11 runtime)

These cells are not MM-STAT-BASE-relative statistics. No shared frontend,
AArch64 generator, linker or future extension may run concurrently with this
phase or allocate these cells while the optional x86 route owns them.

CODE packs eight little-endian bytes per M word, starting at CODE-BASE+0.
CODE-N counts bytes plus one; byte offset p is word CODE-BASE+floor(p/8),
bits 8*(p mod8)..+7. Capacity is8*CODE-CAP-1 bytes. The packed route never
passes CODE-N or CODE contents to the AArch64 generator/layout/output.
FIX records are [disp32-byte-offset, kind, target, 0]:
kind1 FN-relative call, kind2 LABEL-relative branch, kind3 ELF context LEA.
kind4 literal absolute code-relative offset, kind5 RIP-relative literal LEA.
Every displacement and pool offset is checked before patching.
FN FF-CODE is code-byte-offset+1 (zero = unresolved). LABEL packs the SIR
definition index in high32 and code-byte-offset+1 in low32. The prepass rejects
duplicates; relocation reads only low32. Tail admission follows at most64
LABEL/BR continuations through high32 and stops on any effect/value change.
All locations are private to this phase, not AArch64 word indices/fixups.

Guest parameters: rdi,rsi,rdx,rcx,r8. Private context: r9.
Runtime profile parameters6..16: [caller rsp +8*(i-5)] (zero-based i).
The callee reads them at [rbp+16+8*(i-5)]. All call sites align rsp to16.
rbp-8 saves context; local slot s is rbp-8(s+1); temp t is
rbp-8(NSLOTS+2+t). Frames are16-aligned and at most4096 bytes.
The runtime frame adds112 bytes: two scratch words after the declared temps
and a96-byte outgoing argument area. No callee-saved register except rbp is
modified. Single result rax; state results rax/rdx. RES2 preserves rdx across
the preceding call's result move and context reload.
Direct/indirect CALL whose continuation is only LABEL/BR then RET of the same
temp uses leave/jmp when there are at most5 arguments. Stack arguments retain the regular call
path. Source fuel charge sites and callee entry charges are unchanged.

C helpers use the ordinary SysV ABI: rdi=context; rsi,rdx,rcx,r8,r9=operands;
call [rdi+RT-slot]. Context is reloaded from rbp-8 after every helper call and
before every guest call. Pointers/handle validation and allocation budgets
are the existing trusted loader ABI v11's responsibility. No C source
translates Kotoba or emits guest machine instructions.

The ELF profile still rejects handles/runtime operations. The runtime profile
uses an explicitly tagged container, never the architecture-ambiguous KSEED1:
  KX64V1 x86_64-linux sysv-ctx-v11 <payload-length> 0\n\n<payload>
The payload begins with a rel32 jump to main()->i64, then function code and
8-aligned literal data. Strings are pair(code-relative-offset,byte-length),
vectors are v11 handles. The raw payload runs under the trusted x86_64 loader
with offset0/arity0; the Python harness only verifies and copies bytes.
The runtime fuel budget comes from that loader's context, not the container.
The optional Linux bootstrap launcher includes the pinned C loader unchanged
and pre-touches512 KiB of its existing stack before sandbox installation.
The unchanged loader lowers RLIMIT_AS after mapping its fixed arenas, so
post-sandbox heap/stack growth would otherwise fail. glibc reserves16 MiB
of ordinary allocator headroom at startup; the stack reserve stays below
the existing1 MiB stack budget. No syscall grant or process limit is widened.

X86_64-LINUX-MANIFEST retains all adopted AArch64/project routes in the
optional AArch64 cross compiler. X86_64-LINUX-SELFHOST-MANIFEST builds a
source-only Linux compiler with the same reader/checker/lowerer and x86
backend. Its driver requires the explicit x86 target and format; it refuses
project/KIR/source-path/policy routes rather than discarding their flags.
Both profiles reject floating-point signatures/operations before output.
State templates have no standalone SIR and are skipped exactly as lw-fns
requires; all concrete specializations are admitted. Generated closure
adapters have no parameter type table; their checked lambda is admitted.
No externally callable scalar ABI, signed artifact admission or Q9 claim.

## Kernel object route (x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1), seed module 74

`compile SOURCE --target x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1 --output NEW_OBJ [--fuel
POSITIVE_BUDGET]` emits the aiueos kernel object ABI (ADR-0356): ET_REL, the
seven sections `"" .text .data .rela.text .symtab .strtab .shstrtab`, the
source's single export as one GLOBAL FUNC in `.text`, one 80-byte private
context in `.data` (second quadword = the per-call fuel bound), and exactly
one `R_X86_64_PC32` addend -4 relocation of the wrapper's `lea r9,[rip+.data]`
against the `.data` symbol. The wrapper is lea, fuel replenish,
`sub rsp,8`, call entry, `add rsp,8`, `ret`. Admission is the profile-0 scalar
subset plus the one-export rule; `--fuel` over 2^31-1 takes the imm64
replenish path and stays ABI-identical. This route emits the object ABI only:
not the kernel image, not the entry allowlist, not the fuel-tier table, and no
emitted object has run in a kernel.

## Loaderless static image (x86_64-linux-static), seed module 75

`compile SOURCE --target x86_64-linux --output NEW_ELF --format static` emits
one ELF64 ET_EXEC (EM_X86_64, no PT_INTERP/PT_DYNAMIC, no libc) holding the
guest and a runtime written in Kotoba (`xs-rt-src`). The runtime text is
appended after the guest (and its r6b library) and compiled by the same
frontend and runtime-profile backend. Absolute header cells, static builds
only: 197 = byte offset of the runtime text (0 = not a static build), 198 =
first intrinsic FN, 199 = `kxrt-start`, 200..255 = FN whose absolute address
fills context word slot/8 (slots 48..440). Functions whose symbol starts at or
after cell 197 are runtime functions: no OP-FUEL code, scalar SIR only, and
never called directly by guest functions (E7101). Calls to the eight
`kxrt-` intrinsic stubs (load64, store64, load8, store8, syscall, ctx,
call-main, trap) are replaced by inline code.

Layout: file 0 maps at 0x400000 RX (headers, then code at 0x401000:
entry stub, functions, literal pool); the RW segment at the page-aligned data
offset holds the 400-byte v11 context image (function slots, code_base @384 =
0x401000, code_length @392) plus runtime globals at context+1024. Entry:
`mov rdi,rsp; and rsp,-16; call kxrt-start; ud2`. `kxrt-start` maps the
arenas, fills version/data slots/fuel/allow, installs SIGILL/TRAP/BUS/FPE/SEGV
handlers, calls `main` as the loader does (`fn(0,0,0,0,0, ctx)`, r9 =
context), closes the append sink, flushes stdout and exits with
`result & 0xff` (command mode), or prints the structured line when
`KEXE_STRUCTURED_REPORT` is set. Environment read: `KEXE_ALLOW` (default
35,37,38,39; `-` = none), `KEXE_FUEL`, `KEXE_STRUCTURED_REPORT`,
`KEXE_CAP_RESOURCES_35` (one absolute root; opens use openat2
RESOLVE_BENEATH|RESOLVE_NO_MAGICLINKS), `KEXE_CPU_SECONDS`, `KEXE_WALL_SECONDS`,
`KEXE_SECCOMP`. Before the guest runs: an alternate signal stack (64 KiB,
SA_ONSTACK) and SIGALRM/SIGXCPU/SIGSYS handlers; 960 KiB of stack pre-touched,
then RLIMIT_CORE 0, RLIMIT_CPU (only when `KEXE_CPU_SECONDS` is given; soft s,
hard s+1), RLIMIT_AS 64 MiB, RLIMIT_STACK 1 MiB, alarm(wall-1) for
`KEXE_WALL_SECONDS`; then the loader's seccomp filter: wrong arch kills, the
11-call base set always, the 11-call fs set and the 6 `:fs/app-data` calls only
with an open, granted scope 35, everything else SIGSYS (exit 120). A kernel
without seccomp fails closed (exit 1) unless `KEXE_SECCOMP=if-available`.
`KEXE_SANDBOX_PROBE` issues one filtered call after installation (diagnostic).
Not provided: wire-35 forms other than READ/EXISTS/WRITE/APPEND (refused),
wires other than 35/37/38/39 (refused), the loader's default 1 s cpu / 3 s wall
budgets (no budget unless given), growth regions (pool/item layout differs;
values are equal), the arena fields of the structured line.

### KX64V1 runner (`--format kx64-runner`)

The same image with context word 408 = 1 (link time; 0 for `--format
static`), built from `seed/x86_64-kx64-runner.kotoba`. It takes the trusted
loader's command line: `PAYLOAD OFFSET ARITY ISA ALLOW [I64-ARG ...] [--
GUEST-ARGS]` with ARITY 0..5 and exactly ARITY signed decimal i64 arguments
(the loader's other argument forms, `s:HEX` strings and region references,
exit 2 with a usage line), which enter as the payload's first ARITY argument
registers. The
payload file is read into an anonymous mapping, made read+execute, and
`code_base`/`code_length` are set to it; ALLOW comes from argv and
`KEXE_ALLOW` is ignored. Guest entry is the ninth intrinsic
`kxrt-call-at addr ctx argv` (five argument words at argv; r9 / x7 =
context) at PAYLOAD+OFFSET. With `KEXE_ARENA_USE` set, both formats print the
loader's one-line `KEXE_ARENA_USE {...}` record (17 fields) on stderr at exit
or trap; peaks equal the final use and the growth-region/scope fields are 0. Exit: `KEXE_STRUCTURED_REPORT`
line, else `result & 0xff` when `KEXE_COMMAND` is present, else the signed
decimal result and a newline with status 0, as the loader.

## aarch64-linux-static (seed modules 77, 80)

`compile SOURCE --target aarch64-linux --output NEW --format static|runner`
(Linux selfhost closure; dispatched by 73) emits the same image shape for
EM_AARCH64. Module 80 is a plain stack-machine emitter for the runtime-profile
SIR, independent of the adopted 41-a64gen: every local and temp lives in the
frame (sp-relative, the x86 frame sizes), x0..x4 carry arguments 0..4 and
arguments 5.. go to [sp, 8*(i-5)], x7 holds the context at every fuel site
and guest call, helpers are `ldr x16,[x0,#slot]; blr x16` with x0 = context.
Generated code uses only x0..x17, x29, x30 and sp, so runtime functions are
AAPCS64-callable from a foreign payload. Fixups are MM-FIX kinds 11..17
(imm26 FN/LABEL, imm19 LABEL, absolute FN/data/literal as movz+3 movk,
code-relative literal). Entry: `mov x0, sp; bl kxrt-start; brk #0`. Traps are
`brk #0` (SIGTRAP, exit 120). The runtime (77) is generated from the same
template as 76: syscall numbers, openat for open, setitimer for alarm, O_
flags, AUDIT_ARCH_AARCH64 and the aarch64 allow-lists differ. The runner
format takes `PAYLOAD OFFSET 0 aarch64 ALLOW [-- ARGS]` and enters the payload
as the loader did: fn(0..0, x7 = context). `KEXE_STACK_BYTES` (both arches)
sets the stack budget; the adopted AArch64 compiler needs 8 MiB.
